<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  :feature:antivirus declares exactly one permission, and only because code in THIS module calls the
  API behind it: `AntivirusScanRoute` reads `ConnectivityManager` to report the network gate upward
  as `GateStateReported(isOnline = …)` (docs/screens/15-antivirus.md §1.3 — platform truth lives in
  the composable, MVI §4). `ConnectivityManager.getNetworkCapabilities` throws a SecurityException
  without it.

  The same rule the :data manifest states applies here: only what code in this module actually calls.
  Nothing else is declared on another cluster's or another decision's behalf.

  Deliberately NOT here:
    * The storage read permissions — declared by :data, beside MediaStoreQuery, which is the code
      that uses them. This module only *asks* for them through an ActivityResultContract.
    * MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE — never assumed grantable (system-architecture.md §8.1, owner
      decision). The scan degrades to ScanCoverage.InstalledAppsOnly and SAYS SO instead.
    * QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES — this module enumerates no packages. The result rows' icons come from
      AppIconLoader and fall back when a package is not visible; a permission a store listing has to
      justify is not declared to improve an icon.
    * DELETE_PACKAGES — it satisfies the same gate REQUEST_DELETE_PACKAGES below does, and its
      protection level is signature|privileged, so no installable build of this app can hold it.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      `AntivirusResultRoute` launches Intent.ACTION_DELETE for a flagged row (docs/screens/15 §2.5),
      so this module declares the permission that intent needs, on the same "only what code in THIS
      module calls" rule as the line above.

      THIS LINE USED TO SAY THE OPPOSITE — that ACTION_DELETE "is the system dialog and needs no
      permission" — and that claim is what broke both uninstall call sites in this app. The framework
      documents the requirement only against ACTION_UNINSTALL_PACKAGE, but the real gate is inside
      the system uninstaller, applies to ACTION_DELETE just the same, and keys off targetSdk (36):

          // com.android.packageinstaller.UninstallerActivity, decompiled from
          // /system/priv-app/GooglePackageInstaller/ on RF8Y60B9NCZ (SM-A165F, Android 16)
          if (getMaxTargetSdkVersionForUid(uid) >= 28
              && checkPermission("android.permission.REQUEST_DELETE_PACKAGES", 0, uid) != 0
              && checkPermission("android.permission.DELETE_PACKAGES", 0, uid) != 0) {
              setResult(1); finish(); return;
          }

      It fails silently and nothing throws: the intent resolves, the uninstaller starts, forwards to
      its bottom sheet, and that activity finishes inside onCreate before drawing. The launcher still
      returns, so the row's in-flight flag is lowered and the screen looks like it asked and was
      refused. Measured on RF8Y60B9NCZ, uid 10038 being this app:

          E UninstallRepository: Uid 10038 does not have
              android.permission.REQUEST_DELETE_PACKAGES or android.permission.DELETE_PACKAGES

      Normal (install-time) permission: no runtime dialog, and unlike QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES no Play
      declaration form. :feature:files declares it too, for AppManagerRoute's own ACTION_DELETE.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_DELETE_PACKAGES" />

</manifest>
